WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.210
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:09.450
With the recent ascendancy of neural network-based
techniques, we have witnessed amazing algorithms

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:15.300
that are able to take an image from a video
game and translate it into reality and the

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:16.499
other way around.

00:00:16.499 --> 00:00:22.069
Or, they can also translate daytime images
to their nighttime versions, or change summer

00:00:22.069 --> 00:00:24.720
to winter and back.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:30.140
Some AI-based algorithms can also create near
photorealistic images from our sketches.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:34.390
So the first question is, how is this wizardry
even possible?

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:40.050
These techniques are implemented by using
Generative Adversarial Networks, GANs in short.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:44.690
This is an architecture where two neural networks
battle each other: the generator network is

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:48.520
the artist who tries to create convincing,
real-looking images.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:53.590
The discriminator network is the critic that
tries to tell a fake image from a real one.

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:57.870
The artist learns from the feedback of the
critic and will improve itself to come up

00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:03.390
with better quality images, and in the meantime,
the critic also develops a sharper eye for

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:04.820
fake images.

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:09.790
These two adversaries push each other until
they both become adept at their tasks.

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:13.920
However, the training of these GANs is fraught
with difficulties.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.770
For instance, it is not guaranteed that this
process converges to a point, and therefore

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:22.750
it matters a great deal when we stop training
the networks.

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:28.079
This makes reproducing some works very challenging
and is generally not a desirable property

00:01:28.079 --> 00:01:29.079
of GANs.

00:01:29.079 --> 00:01:34.679
It is also possible that the generator starts
focusing on a select set of inputs and refuses

00:01:34.679 --> 00:01:39.750
to generate anything else, a phenomenon we
refer to as mode collapse.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:42.560
So how could we possibly defeat these issues?

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:47.570
This work presents a technique that mimics
the steps of evolution in nature: evaluation

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:50.139
and selection and variation.

00:01:50.139 --> 00:01:55.719
First, this means that not one, but many generator
networks are trained, and only the ones that

00:01:55.719 --> 00:02:00.189
provide sufficient quality and diversity in
their images will be preserved.

00:02:00.189 --> 00:02:05.310
We start with an initial population of generator
networks, and evaluate the fitness of each

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:06.310
of them.

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:11.250
The better and more diverse images they produce
more fit they are, the more fit they are,

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:16.540
the more likely they are to survive the selection
step where we eliminate the most unfit candidates.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:22.220
Okay, so we now see how a subset these networks
become the victim of evolution.

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:24.640
This is how networks get eaten, if you will.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:26.750
But, how do we produce new ones?

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:31.410
And this is how we arrive to the variation
step, where new generator networks are created

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:36.360
by introducing variations to the networks
that are still alive in this environment.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:40.510
This simulates the creation of an offspring,
and will provide the next set of candidates

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:45.541
for the next selection step, and we hope that
if we play this game over a long time, we

00:02:45.541 --> 00:02:47.830
get more and more resilient offsprings.

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:52.620
The resulting algorithm can be trained in
a more stable way, and it can create new bedroom

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:55.770
images when being shown a database of bedrooms.

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:59.980
When compared to the state of the art, we
see that this evolutionary approach offers

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:03.880
higher quality images and more diversity in
the outputs.

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:07.350
It can also generate new human faces that
are quite decent.

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:12.180
They are clearly not perfect, but a technique
that can pull this off consistently will be

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:16.770
an excellent baseline for newer and better
research works in the near future.

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:21.800
We are also getting very close to an era where
we can generate thousands of convincing digital

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:25.550
characters from scratch to name just one application.

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:26.960
What a time to be alive!

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:29.799
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

